  "text": "Session covered: implementing /cc-track:fix-issues command (completed earlier), then shifted to analyzing token usage patterns. Key insights:\n\n- Craig runs 4 parallel Claude Code terminals for cc-track workflows, letting specs that would take humans weeks complete in ~1 hour with minimal supervision\n- December cost ~$820 in 7 days, mostly Opus 4.5 (89% of spend)\n- Haiku subagents now 9% of spend vs 1% before - delegating work down successfully\n- Cost per 1K output tokens: $0.35 (Sonnet era) vs $1.13 (Opus era) - 3x increase but better quality\n\nCreated backup system for ccusage data:\n- /home/ubuntu/projects/cc-cost/backup-usage.sh - weekly cron dumps to data/\n- Keeps backups indefinitely (Craig's preference over 90-day retention)\n\nClaude Code 2.0.64 release notes discussed:\n- \"Made auto-compacting instant\" - huge for long-running workflows\n- Agents can wake main thread asynchronously - fixes subagent completion detection issues\n- .claude/rules/ with glob patterns - new capability for path-specific instructions\n- /stats now has usage graphs built in\n\nFor cc-track: .claude/rules/ main new capability is glob matching (paths: **/*.test.ts). Rest formalizes what @imports do. Wait for plugin rules support, then split: plugin handles \"how to use cc-track\", project handles \"codebase specifics\".",
  "sections": [
    "Project Notes"
  ],
